        <div id="t3s-plus" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Yesoul T3S Plus Review: What the Figures Say</h2>
<p>
            The T3S Plus is the Yesoul with the most reviews on retailer sites, and the one most people mean by
            "Yesoul treadmill". It is easiest to understand as a compact folding walking and jogging treadmill
            with an unusually large screen.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The screen is the point</h3>
          <p>
            A 21.5-inch 1080p screen that rotates for floor workouts is the kind of feature that usually comes
            with a $1,500 to $2,500 treadmill and a monthly membership. On the T3S Plus it mirrors your phone by
            cable, which means you can run Netflix, YouTube, Zwift-style apps or a rival brand’s classes on it.
            Yesoul states that mirroring is free, unlimited and needs no paid Yesoul membership, but that it
            requires a phone with video output over the cable: Android phones need DisplayPort support, and
            Yesoul lists some recent iPhone models as not supported for wired mirroring. Check your phone against
            Yesoul’s list before buying.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The deck and motor are modest</h3>
          <p>
            Yesoul lists a 45.3 by 17.7-inch running surface. That is fine for walking and an easy jog, but short
            for running at pace: most runners need 55 to 60 inches, as our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a> explains. The 8.7 mph top speed is a 6:54 mile, so intervals are limited too. The motor is rated
            3.0 HP "max", a peak figure; Yesoul does not publish a continuous rating for the T3S Plus.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The incline is genuinely useful</h3>
          <p>
            A powered 10% incline is the T3S Plus’s best mechanical feature for walkers. Incline walking raises
            the effort of a walk without the impact of running, and 10% covers most incline-walking workouts,
            though it falls short of the 12% the 12-3-30 calls for. Our guide to <a href="/what-incline-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what incline to walk at</a> sets out how to use it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What owners and reviewers say</h3>
          <p>
            Independent coverage is thin. Consumer Reports includes the T3S Plus in its treadmill ratings, but
            its scores are for subscribers only and we have not seen them. Search results for Walmart’s marketplace
            listing showed only a handful of mixed reviews, and one of Best Buy’s listings showed none. Yesoul’s own page shows a large
            review count, but those reviews are collected and displayed by Yesoul, so we do not treat them as
            independent evidence. If you want a machine with years of owner reports
            behind it, look at the established brands on our <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under $1,000 list</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="t5-series" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Yesoul T5 Series: Plus, Max and Ultra</h2>
<p>
            The T5 series, added to Yesoul’s US store in June 2026, is a different kind of machine from the T3S.
            All three share the same frame, motor and deck: a 20 by 60-inch running surface, a brushless motor
            rated 4.5 HP peak and 2.0 HP continuous, 0.5 to 12.4 mph, a 350 lb capacity and an 11-inch deck
            height, folding vertically with a hydraulic soft drop. The difference between the three is the
            console and the grade range.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>T5 Plus ($1,399.99):</strong> incline up to 30%, a 21.5-inch swivel screen that mirrors your phone by cable.</li>
            <li><strong>T5 Max ($1,599.99):</strong> adds a 21.5-inch touchscreen with native apps and streaming, and an AI camera for form cues with a physical shutter.</li>
            <li><strong>T5 Ultra ($1,799.99):</strong> adds a -3% decline, with the deck physically tilting downhill, and a built-in fan.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>A 30% incline is rare.</strong> Most home treadmills top out at 12 to 15%; only incline
            trainers such as NordicTrack’s X series go higher. Steep incline lets you raise your heart rate at a
            walking pace, which is why Yesoul markets the T5 around Zone 2 training. Its ZoneFinder feature
            estimates a personal Zone 2 heart-rate range from your workouts, and ZoneSync adjusts the speed to keep
            you in it, using the chest strap or a supported watch. Our guide to <a href="/zone-2-training-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zone 2 training on a treadmill</a> explains the method, and our list of <a href="/treadmills-with-the-highest-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills with the highest incline</a> shows where 30% sits.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A continuous motor rating.</strong> Yesoul publishes 2.0 HP continuous alongside the 4.5 HP
            peak figure on the T5 pages. That is less than the 3 to 4 CHP that full-size running treadmills from
            mainstream brands typically quote, but it is an honest, comparable number, which most budget brands do
            not give. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a> sets out what each continuous rating suits.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Which to choose.</strong> If you already watch on a phone or tablet, the T5 Plus does the
            training for $200 less than the Max. Buy the Max for a self-contained touchscreen, and the Ultra only
            if decline matters to you, for example for a hilly race.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-returns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Yesoul Warranty, Home Trial and Returns</h2>
<p>
            Yesoul’s terms differ sharply between the T3S and T5 lines, and both depend on buying from Yesoul’s
            own store.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">T3S Plus</h3>
          <p>
            The T3S Plus carries a 12-month limited warranty. Yesoul’s product page offers a 14-day home trial
            from delivery: contact Yesoul within 14 days for a return or exchange. For a return that is not a
            quality fault, you pay the return shipping and a $100 restocking fee, and the treadmill must go back
            in its original packaging with all accessories, new and unused. Yesoul also offers free cancellation
            or return within 24 hours of placing the order. Faults found later are handled under the warranty
            after Yesoul’s technical team has assessed them.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">T5 series</h3>
          <p>
            The T5 models carry a much longer warranty: five years on the frame, three on the motor and 12 months
            on parts and labour, according to Yesoul’s specification page, with the same five, three and one-year
            split in Sole’s independent comparison. The home trial is 30 days from delivery, but a non-quality
            return carries a $300 return shipping fee, and paid delivery and installation fees are not refunded.
            Keep the box and internal packaging until you are sure. If the T5’s price drops within 30 days of your
            order, Yesoul says it will refund the difference on request.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Delivery and assembly</h3>
          <p>
            Standard delivery is free. The T5 arrives about 80% pre-assembled, and Yesoul estimates an hour to
            finish it; white-glove delivery to the room of your choice, with assembly, costs $299.99 per machine
            where available. Standard delivery is to the first sheltered place, with no stairs. At about 222 lb
            assembled and 261 lb boxed, plan the route in; our guide to <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-weigh/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill weights</a> helps. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a> explains how to compare Yesoul’s terms with other brands’.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="membership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Do You Need a Yesoul Membership?</h2>
<p>
            No. This is the main thing that distinguishes Yesoul from the subscription-led brands. On the T3S
            Plus, wired mirroring from your phone is free and unlimited, and the Yesoul app account you need to
            register is free. On the T5 Max and Ultra, the touchscreen runs apps and streaming natively. Yesoul
            does sell an optional paid membership for its own guided content; Sole’s 31 October comparison gives
            it as $69.99 a year or $99.99 for lifetime access, a figure we could not confirm on Yesoul’s store on
            the day.
          </p>
          <p>
            Compare that with NordicTrack and ProForm, whose touchscreens are built around an iFIT membership, or
            Peloton’s, which needs All-Access for classes. Our analysis of <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether iFIT is worth it</a> covers the cost of the subscription model over a few years. For many buyers, a screen that simply
            shows what is on their phone is worth more than a library of classes they will not use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Recalls and Safety</h2>
<p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission’s recall database for Yesoul on 5 November 2026
            and found no recall of any Yesoul treadmill or other product. The treadmill recalls since 2015 concern
            other brands, including Peloton, Nautilus, Horizon and Matrix.
          </p>
          <p>
            A 30% incline and a -3% decline change the hazards of a treadmill. At steep grades the belt pulls
            harder against a stumble, and the rear of the deck rises well off the floor, so keep the space behind
            the machine clear, use the safety key every time, and keep children and pets away from the rear
            roller. Our <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety guide</a> covers the rest. If you use the T5 Max or Ultra’s camera, close its shutter when you are not training.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy a Yesoul Treadmill, and the Alternatives</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The T3S Plus suits</strong> walkers and joggers who want a large screen for their own apps
            without a subscription, in a machine that folds, at a price below most screen treadmills. It suits
            less well runners, taller users, and anyone who wants a warranty beyond a year.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The T5 series suits</strong> buyers who want steep incline walking, a full-size deck that
            also takes running, and freedom from subscriptions, and who are comfortable with a new product line
            that has little independent track record yet. Use the 30-day trial seriously.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The alternatives.</strong> For a big screen with structured classes, compare the T3S Plus
            with the machines on our <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best smart treadmills</a> list. For steep incline, an incline trainer or a mainstream treadmill with 15% incline from our <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline treadmills</a> list is the established option. And for a subscription-free treadmill with a long record, look at the
            brands on our <a href="/best-treadmill-brands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill brands</a> page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Yesoul Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Yesoul’s machines are easy to misjudge because the screens are so prominent. These are the four mistakes that matter most.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying the T3S Plus to run on</h3>
              <p>A 45.3-inch belt and 8.7 mph top speed suit walking and jogging. Runners, especially taller ones, will hit the ends of the deck and the speed limit. If you want to run, the T5 series is the Yesoul built for it.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not checking your phone works with the screen</h3>
              <p>The T3S Plus and T5 Plus mirror by cable, which needs video output from the phone. Some Android phones and some recent iPhones are not supported. Check Yesoul’s compatibility notes before you order.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Treating the stated price as the real one</h3>
              <p>Yesoul shows a higher "regular" price beside almost everything, and retailers have sold the T3S Plus well below it. Compare the price you would pay with what similar machines sell for, not with the crossed-out figure.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Throwing the box away during the trial</h3>
              <p>Both home trials require the original packaging, and non-quality returns cost $100 plus shipping on the T3S Plus and $300 on the T5. Keep the box and inner packing until the trial ends.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Yesoul is worth a look for a big, subscription-free screen at a modest price
          or for a steep 30% incline on a full-size deck, provided you buy direct, use the trial and accept that
          independent long-term evidence is thin.</strong> The T3S Plus suits walkers and joggers; the T5 Plus is
          the T5 most buyers need; the Max and Ultra add a touchscreen and decline.
          </p>
          <p>
            Compare any Yesoul with the machines on our <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best smart treadmills</a> list, and read the <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">return policies guide</a> before deciding where to buy.
          </p>
